The keychain call that never returns names its own boundary now.

MoneyMoney reaches its startup screen and stops. A keychain query fans out to two back ends and
merges them, so "SecItemCopyMatching did not return" named four candidates at once and none of the
logs above could separate them. CIDER_TRACE_SECITEM prints one line per boundary, and a missing exit
line IS the finding. MEASURED:

    CIDER_SECITEM CopyMatching enter ios=1 osx=1
    CIDER_SECITEM CopyMatching_ios call
    CIDER_SECITEM CopyMatching_ios back status=-34018
    CIDER_SECITEM CopyMatching_osx call
    CIDER_SECITEM osx params call
    CIDER_SECITEM osx attrlist call
    CIDER_SECITEM osx attrlist back
    CIDER_SECITEM osx SearchCreateFromAttributes call list=default
    CIDER_SSCLNT findSecurityd call
    CIDER_SSCLNT findSecurityd back port=0xd03
    CIDER_SSCLNT verifyPrivileged2 call
    <nothing, ever>

So the MODERN back end, secd over XPC, answers PROMPTLY with errSecMissingEntitlement, and the
LEGACY osx path is the one that blocks: the bootstrap lookup for com.apple.SecurityServer SUCCEEDS
and hands back a real port, and the FIRST MIG call to that port never gets a reply. MIG has no
timeout, so the caller waits forever with nothing on any log.

THAT CORRECTS TWO THINGS I WROTE DOWN. secd is not the blocker and never was. And the securityd
abort recorded as a side story is exactly where this lands, so it is not a side story.

The instrument prints WHICH back end and WHAT OSStatus, and never a query or a result. That is
deliberate rather than incidental: the application it diagnoses talks to banks, and an instrument
that printed keychain contents would be a worse bug than the one it chases.

